<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
    Direct-distribution profile. There is deliberately NO
    com.apple.security.app-sandbox key:

    - The cleaner needs to enumerate ~/Library/Caches, ~/Library/Logs and
      DerivedData directly.
    - Full Disk Access (TCC kTCCServiceSystemPolicyAllFiles) only grants real
      root-level visibility — for the treemap and the forensic security tier —
      to NON-sandboxed processes.
    - The security division spawns the bundled Python engine as a child
      process, which inherits this process's TCC grants.

    Sign with Hardened Runtime (codesign \-\-options runtime). For public
    distribution, sign with a Developer ID identity and notarize.
    -->

    <!--
    Required by the macOS 11 / 10.15 builds. Those targets ship
    Contents/Frameworks/libswift_Concurrency.dylib (the toolchain's Swift
    Concurrency back-deployment runtime), because /usr/lib/swift only gained
    that dylib in macOS 12.

    The Hardened Runtime turns on *library validation*, which refuses to map
    any dylib that is neither a platform binary nor signed with the same Team
    ID as the main executable. A locally signed build (self-signed or ad-hoc)
    has no Team ID at all, so AMFI rejects the bundled runtime — "mapped file
    has no Team ID and is not a platform binary" — and dyld aborts at the first
    `actor` / `async` call with:

        can't resolve symbol _swift_defaultActor_initialize … because dependent
        dylib @rpath/libswift_Concurrency.dylib could not be loaded

    macOS 12+ never hit this: there /usr/lib/swift wins the rpath search and
    the bundled copy is never mapped, which is why only 11 and 10.15 crashed.
    Disabling library validation is Apple's sanctioned answer for shipping a
    dylib the signing identity does not own; it remains compatible with
    Developer ID signing and notarization.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
